    <desc>Run another program or shell than your default shell in the terminals of a profile.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Custom commands and shells</title>

<p>A <em>shell</em> is software that provides an interface for users of an
  operating system to run <em>commands</em>. The default shell is usually
  <app>Bash</app>. A profile can run another shell, or a command such as
  <cmd>ssh myserver</cmd> or <cmd>top</cmd>, instead:</p>
  <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item><item><p>In the <gui>Profiles</gui> group, click the profile you want to change. Its settings open on a page of their own.</p></item>
<item><p>In the <gui>Command</gui> group, turn on <gui>Use Custom Command</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>In <gui>Custom Command</gui>, type the command with its arguments.</p></item>
<item><p>Open a new tab or window with the profile to try it.</p></item>
</steps>

  <p>The command is run as you type it, with the environment of the terminal.
  You may have to give the full path of the command if its folder is not in
  your <code>PATH</code>.</p>

  <p>You can also <link xref="pref-custom-exit">choose what happens</link>
  when the command exits. <link xref="cmd-shell-integration">Shell
  integration</link> is not added to custom commands.</p>

  <section id="cwd">
    <title>Working folder</title>
    <p><gui>Preserve Working Directory</gui> sets whether new terminals start in
    the working folder of the terminal they are opened from: never, only for
    the shell, or always, also for custom commands.</p>
  </section>

